    <h1>Defense News Classifier</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">An LLM classifier, and the eval harness that kept me honest about it.</p>

    <p class="lead">
      This labels defense-news text on three axes: category (procurement,
      operations, policy, technology, industry), operational domain (air, land,
      sea, cyber, space, multi), and region (indo-pacific, europe, middle-east,
      africa, americas, global). That part is a single model call. The eval
      harness is the interesting part, and it&rsquo;s where data-driven decision
      making actually gets tested: more than once it caught me being wrong, and I
      went with what it showed instead of my own instinct.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-heading">
      <p class="toc-heading" id="toc-heading">On this page</p>
      <ol>
      <li><a href="#why-the-numbers-can-be-trusted">Why the numbers can be trusted</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-the-eval-caught">What the eval caught</a></li>
      <li><a href="#why-an-llm-at-all">Why an LLM at all</a></li>
      <li><a href="#two-ways-of-not-fooling-myself">Two ways of not fooling myself</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-third-axis">The third axis</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-decisions">The decisions</a></li>
      <li><a href="#autonomy-ladder">The ladder it&rsquo;s climbing</a></li>
      <li><a href="#stack">Stack</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-it-demonstrates">What it demonstrates</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="why-the-numbers-can-be-trusted">Why the numbers can be trusted</h2>
    <p>
      The eval has been rebuilt more than once, and the gaps between the versions
      are the honest part. v1 graded the model on 300 snippets it generated itself. Those numbers
      measure consistency, not correctness, and they hid a real hole:
      <code>industry</code> recall was 0.217, the model catching one in five. v2
      replaced that with 54 hand-labeled real snippets (DoD news wire plus SEC
      filings), cross-checked by an Opus judge. On real financial text with actual
      vocabulary, <code>industry</code> went to an F1 of 1.000. A model graded on
      its own output only tells you it&rsquo;s internally consistent. To know
      whether it&rsquo;s actually correct, you need real data that a person
      labeled.
    </p>

    <table class="metrics">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Metric</th><th>v1 synthetic</th><th>v2 real (Sonnet 4.6)</th><th>v2 + Sonnet 5</th><th>v2 + prompt fix</th><th>v3 three-axis (current)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Category accuracy</td><td>79.0%</td><td>88.9%</td><td>88.9%</td><td>94.4%</td><td><span data-metric="category_accuracy">94.4%</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Category macro-F1</td><td>0.765</td><td>0.906</td><td>0.888</td><td>0.950</td><td><span data-metric="category_macro_f1">0.930</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Domain accuracy</td><td>97.3%</td><td>88.9%</td><td>94.4%</td><td>92.6%</td><td><span data-metric="domain_accuracy">98.1%</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Domain macro-F1</td><td>0.973</td><td>0.894</td><td>0.947</td><td>0.932</td><td><span data-metric="domain_macro_f1">0.982</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Region accuracy</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span data-metric="region_accuracy">94.4%</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Region macro-F1</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td><span data-metric="region_macro_f1">0.975</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Every column after the first is the same 54 hand-labeled snippets, so the
      numbers are comparable across the whole row. The domain numbers <em>drop</em>
      from v1 to v2, which is the expected result once the test got harder.
      Synthetic text the model wrote was easy to grade. Real text is harder and
      the lower number is the trustworthy one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The middle column is the migration chapter. In July 2026 the workhorse moved
      from Sonnet 4.6 to Sonnet 5, and the swap went through the same gate as
      every other change here: run the eval before and after, on the same
      snippets. Domain rose to 94.4%; category didn&rsquo;t move. I read that as a
      ceiling made of label ambiguity, which a stronger model can&rsquo;t un-blur.
      I was half right, and the next column is where I found out.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">The ceiling that wasn&rsquo;t</p>
      <p>Calling category a ceiling was a comfortable conclusion, so I went
        looking for the shape of the misses instead of accepting it. A
        per-cell confusion breakdown showed they weren&rsquo;t scattered at all:
        they clustered on one boundary, <code>technology</code> read as
        <code>operations</code> whenever a new system was tested or demoed in an
        operational setting, plus a habit of over-assigning <code>land</code>.
        Two targeted rubric clauses moved category from 90.7% to
        <strong>94.4%</strong> and domain from 90.7% to 92.6%, with
        <code>technology</code> recall reaching 1.000 and nothing regressing.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The lesson:</strong> &ldquo;that&rsquo;s the
        ceiling&rdquo; is a hypothesis, not a finding, and it happens to be the
        one that excuses you from more work. It was a prompt problem wearing a
        ceiling&rsquo;s clothes. Note what it wasn&rsquo;t: a bigger model.</p>
    </div>


    <h2 id="what-the-eval-caught">What the eval caught</h2>
    <p>
      Two moments show why the eval mattered at all.
    </p>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The prompt that read better and performed worse</p>
      <p>I sharpened the system prompt to draw a cleaner line between
        <code>procurement</code> (a purchase, the buyer's view) and
        <code>industry</code> (a company's own business news). It read better to
        me. Then the numbers came in: category accuracy fell from 79.0% to 76.7%,
        and <code>industry</code> recall dropped from 0.217 to 0.100. The sharper
        rule gave the model an even cleaner excuse to dump borderline company
        stories into <code>procurement</code>. I reverted it.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The lesson:</strong> sounding better and
        performing better aren&rsquo;t the same thing. My gut couldn&rsquo;t tell
        the difference here; the numbers could.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" id="decision-grounding" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The grounding that did not pay</p>
      <p>I added BM25 lexical retrieval to ground each classification in similar
        documents, expecting a clear win. Measured lift: +1.9% category accuracy
        (a net of one correct call in 54) and +0.0% on domain, where grounding
        fixed three calls and broke three. Lexical retrieval doesn&rsquo;t earn
        the cost of moving to embeddings here.</p>
      <p>Then it got worse for grounding, and the way I found out is the part
        worth telling. The comparison itself had a bug: I was scoring the
        <em>new-prompt</em> grounded arm against an <em>old-prompt</em> frozen
        baseline, which quietly inflated the lift. Rebuilding the baseline so
        both arms ran the same prompt killed the advantage outright. A
        three-pass confirm settled it: across 162 grounded classifications,
        grounding fixed a domain call <strong>zero</strong> times and broke
        four.</p>
      <p>So I retired it
        (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/012-retire-bm25-grounding.md">ADR-012</a>):
        out of the shipped path, out of the CI gate. The retrieval code, the
        corpus, and the measurement scripts stay in the repo, dormant and
        reproducible, because the built-it-measured-it-cut-it trail is the
        artifact. The verdict hardened from &ldquo;BM25 doesn&rsquo;t justify
        upgrading to embeddings&rdquo; to &ldquo;BM25 doesn&rsquo;t justify
        itself.&rdquo;</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The lesson:</strong> an unfair baseline
        flatters whatever you are hoping for. The first measurement said
        don&rsquo;t bother; a fair one said cut it. Comparing two things means
        changing exactly one of them.</p>
    </div>


    <h2 id="why-an-llm-at-all">Why an LLM at all</h2>
    <p>
      For most of this project&rsquo;s life there was no answer to the question a
      skeptical reader asks in the first thirty seconds &mdash; does this task
      need an LLM? &mdash; which is a conspicuous hole in a repo whose whole
      argument is measure before you spend. So I built the boring baseline:
      TF-IDF into logistic regression, deliberately standard and untuned, scored
      against the same 54 hand-labeled rows as everything else on this page.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The LLM won by 20 points on category and 26 on domain</strong>
      (paired exact McNemar, p=0.013 and p=0.0005). The more useful finding is
      <em>where</em> the baseline broke: it collapses toward whichever class is
      lexically loud, so the LLM is not buying accuracy in general, it is buying
      the boundaries that need reading comprehension. <strong>Two handicaps cut
      against the baseline and both flatter that conclusion</strong> &mdash; its
      training labels came from a judge that itself disagrees with humans 5&ndash;6%
      of the time, and <code>industry</code> had exactly one training row. A
      margin this size survives them; they still get stated.
      <a class="card-link" href="classifier-baseline.html">The full bake-off, handicaps included <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </p>
    <p>
      That claim is the one thing on this page you can check yourself. The
      baseline runs with no key and no server, so it is vendored into this site
      and classifies live in your tab &mdash; pick a row it gets wrong and watch
      which class lights up instead.
      <a class="card-link" href="classifier-baseline.html#try-it">Run the losing baseline <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </p>

    <h2 id="two-ways-of-not-fooling-myself">Two ways of not fooling myself</h2>
    <p>
      Fifty-four hand-labeled snippets is a small set, and a small set has a wide
      error bar. Two follow-ups exist to keep that honest.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Scaling the eval before trusting it</p>
      <p>The 54-snippet gold set carries a 95% confidence interval about 13
        points wide, which is wide enough that a couple of lucky rows look like
        progress. So I scaled to <strong>300</strong> real snippets graded by the
        Opus judge, which roughly halves each interval &mdash; category to
        <strong>93.3%</strong>, domain to <strong>90.3%</strong>. Those
        corroborate the hand-graded numbers rather than replacing them.</p>

      <!-- The scaled-eval Wilson CIs, drawn (portfolio ADR-004 candidate 2).
