      <p class="ci-caption">95% Wilson intervals on an 80&ndash;100% axis. Wide
        bar: the <strong>n=54</strong> human-graded gold the scaled run was
        checked against &mdash; category [84.9, 98.1], domain [82.5, 97.1].
        Narrow bar: the <strong>n=300</strong> judge-graded run &mdash; category
        [89.9, 95.6], domain [86.5, 93.2]. The point estimates barely move; the
        interval is what halves.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The caveat that stays attached:</strong> the
        judge is the answer key here, so this measures agreement with a model
        and inherits its roughly 5&ndash;6% disagreement with humans. The source
        wire is also operations-heavy, which makes the category macro-F1
        uninformative &mdash; the report says so in the report, not in a footnote
        I can quietly drop.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The upgrade that bought nothing</p>
      <p>The roadmap called for tiered routing: escalate the uncertain cases to
        the more expensive model. I built the trigger, measured it, and shipped
        the negative result. Routing moved <strong>+0 rows</strong> on both axes
        at about <strong>1.97&times;</strong> the cost per article. Of nine
        escalations on the gold set it fixed zero and broke one, and even
        sending <em>everything</em> to the premium model scored the same 94.4%
        category, so there was no headroom for any router to capture.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why it&rsquo;s worth the build:</strong> the
        earlier prompt fix had already taken the ground routing was aimed at.
        The cheapest fix won, and now that&rsquo;s measured rather than
        asserted. One more finding stayed in the record because it was
        inconvenient: adding a field to the schema flipped one benign snippet
        into a model refusal, so the measurement apparatus itself changed the
        behavior being measured. Full verdict in
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/013-decline-tiered-routing.md">ADR-013</a>.
        A separate project, measuring
        <a href="faithfulness-judge.html">how reliable an LLM judge is</a>,
        later landed on the same verdict from a different direction &mdash;
        three independent measurements now say escalation barely pays.</p>
    </div>


    <h2 id="the-third-axis">The third axis</h2>
    <p>
      v3 added <code>region</code>, which breaks the output contract on purpose:
      callers that expected two fields now get three. Six labels, with
      <code>global</code> as the single catch-all for stories with no
      geographic anchor and stories spanning several. That catch-all was the
      real design decision, and the alternative (splitting &ldquo;no
      geography&rdquo; from &ldquo;worldwide&rdquo;) would have put the fuzziest
      boundary on the most common kind of story in the corpus. The label set,
      the boundary rules, and the scope limits were written down before the
      build in
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/014-region-field-design.md">ADR-014</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Region landed at 87.0% against fresh hand labels when the axis shipped.
      The more interesting number was that the misses weren&rsquo;t noise:
      <strong>all seven</strong> were stories whose true label is
      <code>global</code> that the model pulled to a specific region, usually
      <code>americas</code>. The rule says label the theater the activity
      happens in, and only when the text says so. The model kept seeing a US
      actor and inferring a US theater, which is the no-guessing rule being hard
      rather than the model being sloppy. One named failure mode beats a
      scattered 13%, because a named one can be worked on.
    </p>
    <p>
      It was worked on, and region now reads
      <strong><span data-metric="region_accuracy">94.4%</span></strong>. The fix
      is one bullet of prompt telling the model that naming a US command or
      contractor identifies the actor, not a place. Getting it shipped took two
      rounds and is the part worth reading: measured once and
      <em>reverted</em> at p=0.0522 against a pre-registered p&lt;0.05, then
      re-run against a ruler twice the size, where the identical clause landed
      at <strong>p=0.0002</strong> and shipped. The bar never moved. The first
      experiment simply never had the power to decide &mdash; about a coin
      flip&rsquo;s worth &mdash; so the honest reading of the revert is
      <em>underpowered</em>, not <em>wrong</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three region misses remain instead of seven, and the error mode inverted:
      only one is still the old cluster, and the other two are the converse
      &mdash; <code>americas</code> rows the clause over-called to
      <code>global</code>. That collateral was priced before the run rather than
      discovered after it. Five under-calls traded for two over-calls is the
      shape of the win, and it is a trade, not a free fix.
    </p>
    <p>
      One number went the other way and is worth stating rather than burying:
      judge-versus-human agreement on region, the measured condition that gated
      the scaled eval in the first place, fell from a perfect
      <strong>100.0%</strong> to <strong>96.3%</strong>. That is 2 disagreements
      in 54 against a CI floor budgeting 3 &mdash; the number that would have
      stopped the release, and the reason the floor exists.
    </p>


    <h2 id="the-decisions">The decisions</h2>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; ADR-002</p>
      <h3>Force structured output with tool-use, not prompt parsing</h3>
      <p>The model is called with a forced tool schema (all three fields required,
        strict enums), so an invalid label is rejected at the API layer before it
        reaches application code. When v3 added <code>region</code>, it was one
        more required strict enum &mdash; the schema is where a new axis goes.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> the schema becomes the contract, and
        the app code stays free of defensive parsing. <strong>Tradeoff:</strong>
        tool-use costs a few extra tokens and a more verbose call signature than
        plain chat. Out-of-enum responses were rare (once in 300) and a single
        re-sample handled them.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Decision</p>
      <h3>One model call, not a multi-step pipeline</h3>
      <p>Classification is a single call, not a generate-reasoning-then-classify
        chain.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> the goal was to measure classifier
        quality honestly, and a simpler baseline makes the eval cleaner to reason
        about. <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> gives up any lift a reasoning step might
        add, which the eval can always be used to revisit later.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; ADR-004</p>
      <h3>Implement the metrics by hand</h3>
      <p>Precision, recall, F1, and confusion matrices are computed directly with
        pandas and arithmetic, no ML framework.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> the call here was to hold off on
        ML tooling until it earns its place, not to default to it because it&rsquo;s
        there. It keeps the dependency surface to <code>anthropic</code> plus
        <code>pandas</code>. <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> about 40 lines that
        <code>sklearn.metrics</code> would replace, accepted for clarity.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; SYS-005 (system-level)</p>
      <h3>In-process, idempotent writeback to the notes API</h3>
      <p>As a component of the larger system, the classifier is invoked by a
        FastAPI BackgroundTask in notes-api and writes labels back as namespaced tags
        with replace semantics, so reprocessing the same note converges instead of
        piling up duplicate tags.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> replace semantics make reprocessing
        safe by design, so idempotency is a requirement, not a nicety.
        <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> the background task carries real retry and
        error-handling logic. See <a href="the-system.html">The System</a> for the
        full loop.</p>
    </div>


    <h2 id="autonomy-ladder">The ladder it&rsquo;s climbing</h2>
    <p>
      This classifier is also the protagonist of the portfolio&rsquo;s roadmap: an
      autonomy ladder. Each level is the <em>same system</em> handed more
      self-direction, not a separate demo. The axis is who drives &mdash; reaching
      for a tool doesn&rsquo;t move you up a level; deciding for yourself does.
    </p>
    <p class="flow-line">
      L1 a human runs it <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span> L2 + a tool
      <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span> L3 it decides it&rsquo;s done
      <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span> L4 agents coordinate
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Every level&rsquo;s story ends with the measurement getting the last
      word.</strong> L2 was climbed and then climbed back down &mdash; the retrieval
      grounding above is that rung, retired. L3&rsquo;s optimisation loop improved
      against the split it could see while giving ground on held-out gold, so it
      was vetoed by the set it never saw. L4 fixed six of the seven rows it was
      aimed at and <em>was still declined</em>: outside its target it broke more
      than it fixed, the repo&rsquo;s first statistically significant harm
      (p=0.016) at four times the cost. Two of the four levels shipped a
      &ldquo;no,&rdquo; and each was written down before it was built.
      <a class="card-link" href="autonomy-ladder.html">The full ladder, level by level <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a>
    </p>

    <h2 id="stack">Stack</h2>
    <p>
      Python, the Anthropic SDK (Sonnet 5 for classification, Opus as the grading
      judge), pandas for the eval tables, FastAPI for the HTTP endpoint, and
      rank-bm25 for the retired grounding experiment. scikit-learn appears in the
      eval dependencies only, for the classical baseline the model was measured
      against; nothing in the shipped path uses it. Tested with pytest on mocked
      calls. The repo is
      public: <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier">github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier</a>
      &mdash; the eval harness, gold set, and ADRs are all checkable, including the
      experiments that were measured and cut.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-it-demonstrates">What it demonstrates</h2>
    <p>
      The dataset is small and the scale is personal. The transferable part is the
      method: evals work like unit tests for an LLM system &mdash; you find out
      before it ships, not after. Over the ladder&rsquo;s climb that method
      measured and declined every escalation offered to it &mdash; retrieval
      three ways, premium-model routing, and a multi-agent review pipeline
      &mdash; so the production system stayed a single well-prompted call, and
      each &ldquo;no&rdquo; is a checkable artifact rather than a taste
      decision. The one &ldquo;yes&rdquo; got the same treatment: the free
      classical baseline that would have made the whole model unnecessary was
      built and measured too, and it lost by 20 points. Declining every
      upgrade is only credible if you also checked the floor.
    </p>
